WEBVTT

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:04.410
Record numbers of immigrants arrive in Britain,

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.090
according to the latest figures.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:10.090
650,000 people came to the UK in the space of a year,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.770
including record numbers from the EU, most of them for work.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.850
We'll be asking how concerned the government will be

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.000
about these latest figures, also this lunchtime.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.400
Football abuse scandal, almost 900 calls in just a week

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.000
to a special helpline as another Newcastle player speaks out.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
I literally just froze, could not speak.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.010
with fear, with shock, with disbelief.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.850
- New footage emerges from the plane

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.930
that crashed carrying a Brazilian football team

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.300
as investigators try to establish why it ran out of fuel.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.160
The UN criticizes Russia for not allowing aid

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.960
into areas of Aleppo as nearly half a million people

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.880
flee the fighting.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.740
Lower speed limits, clean air zones,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.340
and redesigned speed bumps,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.940
how stricter traffic controls could cut deaths

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.000
from pollution.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.600
highly controversial plans to change health services in Dorset

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.400
go out for public consultation and a seven-vehicle pile up in Oxfordshire

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.360
leaves three badly hurt and one man arrested

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.900
Good afternoon and welcome to the BBC News At One.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.740
The number of people who've moved to the UK in a year has hit an all-time high.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.520
Between June 2015 and June 2016, 650,000 migrants arrived here.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.630
That's the equivalent of a city around the size of Glasgow.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.610
The Office for National Statistics said most came here to work

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:53.390
and while one in four were EU citizens, the majority were not.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.670
For the first time, the country which tops the list of migrants is Romania,

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.590
followed by China and Poland.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.720
However, net migration, the difference between immigration and emigration, was 335,000, which

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.540
was similar to the year before.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.060
Sarah Campbell reports.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.430
Much as the government may wish to stem the tide, people keep coming.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:22.530
Britain's status as a place hundreds of thousands want to move to remains unchanged.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:28.080
In the year to June 2016, 650,000 people migrated here.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.000
That's the highest number on record.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.880
They are leaving too. The net migration figure, which takes into account the number who emigrate,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:42.990
is almost unchanged from last year at 335,000. But that's more than three times the government's

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:48.130
aspirational target of less than 100,000 migrants per year.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:52.070
Brexit gives us the opportunity to control numbers coming in from the rest of Europe

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.620
in the same way that we can control numbers coming in from outside Europe. So I think

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.000
there's an opportunity during the negotiations to ensure we can deliver on that, which I

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.600
think was was central to the referendum campaign and a clear message from the

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.640
British people there were a record number of immigrants 284,000 from the

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.570
European Union today's figures also revealed that for the first time Romania

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:18.750
tops the table in terms of country of origin with 54,000 immigrants followed

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:26.630
by 44,000 Chinese and 38,000 Polish very disappointing and underline why we

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:30.000
have to take a very tough position in the

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.160
Brexit negotiations, frankly. I think that migration at the present levels is simply

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.020
unsustainable. We have thought that for some time.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:45.240
Labour today accused the Prime Minister of failing to provide EU nationals with certainty

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:51.020
about their future here. Most of the data released today predates the Brexit vote. However,

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:55.470
there are signs it may be having an impact. In the year to September, there were over

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.000
37,000 grants of permanent residency to Europeans.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.480
double the number the year before.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.940
Those don't include the applications

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.980
that are still being processed by the Home Office

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.900
and there's evidence of quite a big increase

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.120
in the high tens of thousands of applications

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.780
that are still waiting to be decided

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.260
and there's some indication

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.320
that that is going to go up a lot more

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.200
in the quarters to come.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.660
The immigration debate continues to dominate headlines

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.900
and these latest figures will do little

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.050
to quell the concerns of those who are worried

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.000
that too many people see Britain as their destination.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.210
nation of choice. Sarah Campbell, BBC News.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.210
With me now is our Home Affairs correspondent Danny Shaw. How worried is the government

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.010
going to be about these latest figures, record numbers?

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.390
If it's worried, it's certainly not saying that it's worried. It's obviously not happy

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:47.290
that the figures are not going down and they're at historically high levels. But what they

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:52.450
say is that they have an opportunity, when Britain leaves the EU, to get some kind of

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.730
negotiation or some settlement which enables them to control the number of people coming

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.000
in from the European Union. Now, how that

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.660
done well that will be subject to the negotiations because clearly there's

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.740
freedom of movement question. I think what's very interesting about the figures

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.600
is the number of people who are coming to Britain who haven't secured a job but

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.650
are looking for work. Those numbers have gone up to 130,000 from 107,000 the same

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:22.510
period last year. Now is that the key to it? Can the government try and get some

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:27.250
kind of deal so that only EU citizens who've secured employment are able to

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.000
to come here rather than those who are just looking for work.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.280
perhaps that's where the answer to this lies but clearly it's all about those

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.820
negotiations with the EU in the terms of brexit that's the only way

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.760
realistically they can get migration down to the levels that they want it to

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.580
be in the tens of thousands Tanisha thank you our political

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.540
correspondent Carol Walker is at Westminster and Carol leaked documents

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.780
have revealed these controversial plans from Theresa May when she was home

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.740
secretary to deal with the children of illegal immigrants

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.000
that's right what these leaked letters suggest is that Theresa May when she

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.080
was home secretary back in the summer of last year when the government then as now was under

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.860
pressure to control immigration came up with a plan which would essentially de-prioritize the

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.940
children of illegal immigrants. They would be at the back of the queue when it came to school

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:21.800
places. The idea ran into a lot of opposition from ministers amongst them, Nikki Morgan who was

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.740
then education secretary who felt that this could lead to the segregation of these children.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.890
It could mean that some of them might even fall out of education.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.360
altogether and that it would put unfair pressure on schools who'd have to do

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.940
things like carry out checks on passports now some might applaud

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.360
Theresa May for being prepared to take such a tough line to try to control

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.980
immigration but there was so much opposition that the plan was dropped and

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.000
the immigration minister told me this morning that it is not government policy

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.960
and the government has no plans to introduce any sort of scheme like this

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.940
despite all the pressure over those immigration statistics talking of

00:06:58.940 --> 00:06:59.890
government plans

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.560
some detail emerging from David Davis about what Britain could look like

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.070
outside the EU that's right remember back to the referendum campaign those

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.850
who wanted to leave talking about the hundreds of millions of pounds that we

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:17.890
contribute to the EU that we could have back to spend on Britain's own priorities

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:22.450
well today David Davis the minister in charge of the brexit process said Britain

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:27.610
could continue to contribute to the EU even after we leave now that might

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.000
disappoint some people but so far many

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.080
of those leading Brexit campaigners have been pretty relaxed about it they're

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.500
saying that it's right for the government to keep all its options open

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.530
particularly when there's so much uncertainty about exactly what sort of

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:45.860
deal the government can get when it does leave the European Union Carol Walker

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:50.600
in Westminster thank you another former Newcastle United footballer has come

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:54.750
forward to say he was abused while playing for the club David Etox says he

00:07:54.750 --> 00:08:00.000
was groomed by the coach George Ormond when and that when he left the club he was a shell of

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.980
his former self the NSPCC charity said its hotline set up to offer support to

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:10.380
victims has received at least 860 calls in its first week alone our sports

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:16.110
correspondent Richard Conway reports for years football's victims believed they

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:21.560
were alone in their suffering the shocking truth is that they weren't the

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.140
NSPCC's dedicated helpline has confirmed it received over 860 calls in

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:30.000
its first week with more than 60 people referred

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.600
to the police or child services were frankly staggered at the number of calls

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.980
that have been made to this dedicated NSPCC helpline since it was set up a

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:47.680
week ago over 860 now and it is unprecedented in our experience of

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.180
setting up a helpline like this to get so many calls in such a short period of

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:55.860
time the Football Association has launched an internal inquiry into who

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:00.000
knew what and when today the newly appointed England manager

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:12.900
I played with one of the players who's recently come forward and the reality of that, as they have said, is that they haven't felt able to speak about that until this moment.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:20.620
I think the world was very different 20 years ago. I think it is important we acknowledge that and learn from it.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:30.000
And I repeat, I think a lot of those lessons have already been learned because that's why the situation we have now, in terms of child protection, looks completely different.

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:40.570
It's alleged the Premier League side Chelsea made a payment to a former player who claims he was abused by the club's former chief scout in the 1970s.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.530
According to the Daily Telegraph, the money was paid within the past three years.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:51.700
The club have launched their own inquiry amidst reports other big clubs may have made similar payments.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:00.000
The chief executive of the FA was clear when asked today about the potential consequences of clubs engaging in any cover-up.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.930
We have clear laws and rules in the game that need to be followed and if there's been any

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:10.690
evidence of a breach of those, so hush, you know, hushing up would be one, subject to

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:15.680
due process, i.e. the police need to be, you know, the right place in this. When it's

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.840
our turn to apply the rules, we absolutely will, from top to bottom, regardless of size

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.210
of club.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:27.980
Meanwhile, a former Newcastle United footballer, David Ituck, is the latest player to say he

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:30.000
was abused by coach George Orme.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.470
Ormond. Mr Etik was not part of a 2002 case in which Ormond was jailed for six years for

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:41.080
indecent assault, but has now filed a complaint to the police. He says Ormond preyed on him

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:46.310
because he was vulnerable, as his father was seriously ill with bowel cancer at the time.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:52.880
How do you make a statement that you went out with somebody, you came back to your room,

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:59.280
they masturbated in front of you? How do you tell that story without feeling like you're

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.660
As more players come forward and authorities continue to investigate, British football

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.020
is waking up to the fact there will be no quick fix to this crisis.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.190
Richard Conway, BBC News.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:16.510
Health experts are urging councils in England to consider new speed limits for road traffic

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.030
to improve air quality in urban areas.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:24.370
The health advisory body, NICE, says pollution largely from traffic contributes to 25,000

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.270
deaths in England each year.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.390
Our health correspondent, Robert Piggott, reports.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.500
The smoky veil masking the dawn over London contains the pollution that in one year alone

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.520
killed nearly 10,000 of its people.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.840
Across the country, pollution from road traffic, nitrogen dioxide and tiny particles from vehicle

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:49.520
exhaust brake linings and tyres are causing cancer and respiratory and heart disease.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:55.820
Coping with the impact on health costs the UK up to £18bn a year.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:12:00.000
Nice says a change in traffic flow could make the air significantly

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.770
cleaner we're really encouraging local authorities reduce the environments who

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:11.110
encourage more smoother driving less speeding less acceleration and

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:16.760
deceleration which is what causes the emission of these these harmful products

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:22.260
road traffic has increased to record levels estimates for the 12 months ending

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:28.210
in September 2016 suggests there were 320 billion vehicle miles traveled on

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.000
British roads almost one and

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.440
and a half percent higher than the previous 12 months.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.920
Nice says smoother driving would come

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.440
with more variable speed limits on motorways

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:43.040
and 20 mile an hour speed limits in congested streets.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.400
Another suggestion is for speed humps to be redesigned

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.840
to reduce acceleration between them,

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.560
although safety groups argue that they also reduce accidents.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:56.360
Cheaper housing is often near the busiest roads,

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:00.000
so pollution disproportionately affects poorer people.

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.660
NICE wants councils to distance new houses from traffic

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:09.240
and to site the most heavily used rooms in them away from the road.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:12.750
Almost everything that's in the guidelines requires more money.

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:15.480
It can be revenue funding, day-to-day expenditure,

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.840
or it can be capital funding for new assets.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.240
So if we want to see the sort of action that NICE are proposing,

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.260
the government really is going to have to step in

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:24.580
and address some of those funding issues.

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:26.980
The guidelines aren't mandatory,

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:30.000
but they may increase pressure on councils to act

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:35.060
give them moral support for potentially unpopular measures. Robert Piggott, BBC

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:40.960
News. New images have emerged from onboard the plane that crashed, killing

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:44.140
most of a Brazilian football team. Investigators are trying to establish

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:48.100
why the plane ran out of fuel as it came into land. There was also a

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:53.440
catastrophic electrical failure. Only six of the 77 people on board survived.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:14:00.000
Our correspondent Richard Lister reports. The last images of the

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.760
passengers and crew on La Mia Flight 2933. Everything is normal. The pilot is clearly

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.160
thrilled to have the Capicoense team on board.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:15.360
They're going with a lot of hope and a lot of luck, says Miguel Quiroga. I think they'll

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.270
get good results.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:26.840
His co-pilot, Cissi Arias, talks of her pride that a Brazilian team has chosen their airline

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.000
to fly to Medellin.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:36.150
But just a few hours later, almost everyone on board became the victim of an appalling tragedy.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:40.860
The plane never reached its destination. All but six people on board were killed.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:45.860
The 71 bodies have been transported from the crash site as investigators try to understand

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:50.860
what happened here. The lack of any explosion was an indication that the plane had little

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:56.370
or no fuel on board, and the pilot seems to have confirmed that in his final transmissions.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:11.250
Lamia 933 is in total electrical failure. He says no fuel. He pleads for an immediate

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:15.200
landing, citing a fuel emergency.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:19.640
After taking off from Santa Cruz in Bolivia, the airline director says the pilot had the

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:25.740
option of refueling in Bogota, but he chose to carry on to Medellin. He crashed just minutes

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:30.000
from the airport.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.330
One thing we are investigating is the engine stopped due to a lack of fuel, and without

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:43.060
fuel the electrical power would have been completely lost.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:47.520
In the Chapicoense home stadium last night, a memorial service for the team.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:52.440
They were killed on their way to what would have been the biggest match of their lives.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.290
But it's here that their coffins will be brought for the funeral service.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:57.710
Richard Lister, BBC News.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:00.000
Let's speak to our transport crew.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.080
correspondent Richard Westcott it seems extraordinary that a plane can just run

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.380
out of fuel like that it does we still don't have all the facts but there are

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:11.500
some obvious critical questions I mean you heard Richard there say it was due

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:15.550
to refuel on route well why didn't that happen that's the first critical

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:21.070
question secondly it's clear that it's circling for about 13 minutes before it

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:24.370
comes in to land only about three or four minutes from landing at the airport

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:28.090
so why has it been told to circle well apparently another aircraft was given

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:30.000
PRIORITY TO LAND AHEAD OF IT.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:32.180
because that aircraft had a problem as well.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:33.720
So they'll now be asking,

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.280
why didn't the crew make it clearer, perhaps,

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.040
that they had a crisis on their hands?

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:40.800
Didn't they realise just how bad the situation was?

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:42.680
Because 13 minutes of circling,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.760
well, that could be the critical amount of time

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:45.710
that could have got them down.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.550
So there's still lots to come out.

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.450
They've still got to analyse what's known as the black boxes.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:51.610
They're doing that in the UK.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.510
They'll be in the UK by the weekend or next week,

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.790
and they'll be going through that to really get at the facts.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.000
But critical questions, really, on decisions taken,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.460
potentially by the crew and by air traffic controllers that could

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:06.600
theoretically have stopped this catastrophe extraordinary even when you

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.600
just look at those pictures that anyone survived it absolutely what is

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:12.500
interesting about those pictures for an air crash actually the aircraft is quite

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:16.560
intact and the pieces are quite big now that possibly suggests I've been talking

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:20.580
to pilots that the pilot was trying to sort of glide it in rather than hit the

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:24.420
ground very very hard so potentially perhaps that's why just a few people

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:30.000
survived Richard thank you the time is just after quarter past one our top story this lunch

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.990
The latest figures say immigration to the UK reached its highest ever level in the year

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:40.210
running up to the EU referendum.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:45.350
